Capitol Records' rejection of the group's recordings in the US was now [[Brian Epstein]]'s primary concern, and he encouraged [[Lennon–McCartney|Lennon and McCartney]] to write a song to appeal specifically to the American market.{{sfn|MacDonald|1998|pp=88}} [[George Martin]], however, had no such explicit recollections, believing that Capitol was left with no alternative but to release "I Want to Hold Your Hand" due to increasing demand for the group's product.<ref name=YouTube_iim6s8Ea_bE>{{cite web |title=George Martin introduces "I Want To Hold Your Hand" video |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iim6s8Ea_bE |via=YouTube |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121102141516/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iim6s8Ea_bE |archive-date=2 November 2012}}</ref>

McCartney had recently moved into 57 [[Wimpole Street]], London, where he was lodging as a guest of Dr [[Richard Asher|Richard]] and Margaret Asher, and whose daughter, actress [[Jane Asher]], had become McCartney's girlfriend earlier in the year. This location briefly became Lennon and McCartney's new writing base, taking over from McCartney's Forthlin Road home in [[Liverpool]].{{sfn|Miles|1997|pp=107}} Margaret Asher taught the oboe in the "small, rather stuffy music room" in the basement{{sfn|Miles|1997|pp=107}} where Lennon and McCartney sat at the piano and composed "I Want to Hold Your Hand". In September 1980, Lennon told ''[[Playboy]]'' magazine:

{{blockquote|We wrote a lot of stuff together, one on one, eyeball to eyeball. Like in 'I Want to Hold Your Hand,' I remember when we got the chord that made the song. We were in Jane Asher's house, downstairs in the cellar playing on the piano at the same time. And we had, 'Oh you-u-u/ got that something{{nbsp}}...' And Paul hits this chord and I turn to him and say, 'That's it!' I said, 'Do that again!' In those days, we really used to absolutely write like that—both playing into each other's noses.<ref>{{cite web |title=With The Beatles |work=The Beatles Interview Database |url=http://www.beatlesinterviews.org/dba02with.html |access-date=1 September 2004}}</ref>}}

In 1994, McCartney agreed with Lennon's description of the circumstances surrounding the composition of "I Want to Hold Your Hand", saying: "'Eyeball to eyeball' is a very good description of it. That's exactly how it was. 'I Want to Hold Your Hand' was very co-written."{{sfn|Miles|1997|pp=108}} According to [[Ian MacDonald]], in keeping with how Lennon and McCartney collaborated at that time, lyrically bland, random phrases were most likely called out by the pair; if the phrases fitted the overall sound, they would stay. The song's title was probably a variation of "[[I Wanna Be Your Man]]", which the Beatles had recently recorded at EMI Studios.{{sfn|MacDonald|1998|p=91}}

