Reminiscent of [[Tin Pan Alley]] and [[Brill Building]] techniques and an example of modified [[thirty-two-bar form|32-bar form]],{{sfn|Covach|2005|p=70}} "I Want to Hold Your Hand" is written on a two-[[Bridge (music)|bridge]] model, with only an intervening verse to connect them. The song has no real "lead" singer, as Lennon and McCartney sing alternately in unison and in harmony.

The song is in the key of [[G major]] and lyrically opens two beats early with "''Oh yeah'', I'll tell you something" with a D-B, B-D melody note drop and rise over an I (G) chord.{{sfn|Pedler|2003|p=111}} Controversy exists over the landmark chord that Lennon stated McCartney hit on the piano while they were composing the song. Wolf Marshall considers it to be the minor vi (Em) chord (the third chord in the I–V7–vi (G–D7–Em) progression).<ref>Wolf Marshall. Guitar One. 1966 Vol 6, p16</ref> Walter Everett is of the same opinion.<ref>{{cite book |first=Walter |last=Everett |author-link=Walter Everett (musicologist) |title=The Beatles as Musicians: The Quarry Men Through Rubber Soul |publisher=Oxford University Press |year=2001 |page=110}}</ref> Dominic Pedler claims, however, that more surprising is the melody note drop from B to F{{sharp}} against a III7 (B7) chord on "understand".{{sfn|Pedler|2003|pp=110–111}}

